/* A sigil written in template PROSE is text, not an operator.
 *
 * The node's sigil offset came from a plain `strstr` over its span, on the
 * guarantee that the IR carver had already ensured the only sigil there was
 * real code. Template lowering invalidates that: the template becomes a C
 * string literal inside the same span, so the search found the `!>` in the
 * user's text and spliced `@err` into the middle of it.
 *
 * The script register is the one that shows it. In a `.ccs` template lowering
 * runs before the sigil pass, so the same source is already fine there — which
 * is why this test is a `.shcc`. */
int n = 7;
println(@string(`sigil '!>' and n=${n}`, a)) !>;
println(@string(`bare !> here`, a)) !>;
println(@string(`and ?> too`, a)) !>;
return 0;
